# Collection application

This application runs its checked contract over named collection entries.
The synthesized interface is a plain form. Keys in command and context arrays
are exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits; scalar values are canonical number
strings in interface field order. Review `context.json` before serving.

Create a private file containing a nonzero 64-digit hexadecimal credential.
Set `ZENO_APP_SECRET_FILE` to it, then run:

```text
cargo +1.97.1 run --locked --offline --bin app-server
```

The default address is http://127.0.0.1:8080. Optional settings are
`ZENO_APP_ADDRESS` (loopback only), `ZENO_APP_STORE` (SQLite database) and
`ZENO_APP_CONTEXT` (configured context file). The authenticated principal
selects the fixed context; a request cannot choose one. This profile supports
standalone collection contracts without deliveries or a server clock.

For both issuer and holder roles in one running application, set
`ZENO_APP_PARTIES` to a private JSON file. Each party has exactly `principal`,
`context` and `secret_file`; credential paths are relative to that JSON file:

```json
[
  {"principal":"0707070707070707070707070707070707070707070707070707070707070707","context":["180"],"secret_file":"issuer.secret"},
  {"principal":"0808080808080808080808080808080808080808080808080808080808080808","context":["181"],"secret_file":"holder.secret"}
]
```

The example roles apply to the included token contract. Review the interface for
other contexts. Each credential file has the same private 64-digit format as
above. There may be 1 through 32 parties; configuration files are limited to
64 KiB. Leave `ZENO_APP_SECRET_FILE` and `ZENO_APP_CONTEXT` unset in this mode.
Contexts and credentials are copied at startup. Local recovery reads the party
contexts but does not need the credential files; it replays the historical
contexts recorded in the journal.

`GET /state` returns the revision, scalar state, configured context and interface.
`POST /preview` and `/command` take the existing ingress envelope: byte 1,
32-byte contract ID, 32-byte retry key, big-endian u64 expected revision, then a
JSON array of command strings. Every request carries the credential in its
Authorization header. Preview loads the same named entries and calls the same
Authority as commit. An invalid key receives HTTP 400 with body `Malformed`.

Receipts bind scalar state and every named map root before and after the
commit. The submission, scalar state, map writes and retry index commit in one
SQLite transaction. A restart replays the journal through the same Authority
and checks every stored map before serving. Local recovery commands are:

```text
app-server --journal app.sqlite > journal.json
app-server --replay journal.json restored.sqlite
app-server --audit restored.sqlite
```

Restore requires a new destination and publishes it only after complete replay
and head comparison. The journal profile is `zeno-fcis/collection-journal/2`,
with a 64 MiB input limit. This profile intentionally refuses stores from an
earlier collection-application profile; it supplies no automatic migration.

The form retains an uncertain command for exact retry while the page remains
open. Resolve that command before reloading. The credential is never saved in
browser storage. A key picker, collection listing and durable browser request
recovery are follow-ups. This application profile is separate from the scalar
SQLite publication shell; the collection store is a storage utility and does
not itself supply a library publication capability.

